== Chapter V

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[#emph[The principal Intendment of the whole Discourse proposed. The first main Case of Conscience stated. What it is to Mortify any Sin, negatively considered. Not the utter Destruction of it in this Life. Not the Dissimulation of it. Not the Improvement of any Natural Principle. Not the Diversion of it. Not an Occasional Conquest. Occasional Conquests of Sin, what, and when. Upon the Eruption of Sin, in time of Danger or Trouble.]]
]

#smallcaps[These] things being premised, I come to my principal Intention, of handling some #emph[Questions,] or #emph[practical Cases,,] that present themselves in this business of #emph[Mortification] of Sin in #emph[Believers:] The first, which is the Head of all the rest; and whereunto they are #emph[reduced,] may be considered as lying under the ensuing Proposal.

Suppose a man to be a true #emph[Believer,] and yet finds in himself a powerful Indwelling Sin, leading him captive to the Law of it, consuming his Heart with trouble, perplexing his Thoughts, #emph[weakening his Soul,] as to Duties of Communion with God, disquieting him as to Peace, and perhaps defiling his Conscience, and exposing him to Hardening through the #emph[deceitfulness of Sin]\; what shall he do? What Course shall he take and insist on, for the Mortification of this Sin, Lust, Distemper, or Corruption, to such a Degree, as that though it be not utterly destroyed, yet in his Contest with it, he may be enabled to keep up Power, Strength and Peace, in Communion with God?

In answer to this important Inquiry, I shall do these things.

1\. Show what it is to #emph[mortify] any Sin; and that both #emph[Negatively] and #emph[Positively,] that we be not mistaken in the Foundation.

2\. Give #emph[general Directions] for such things, as without which it will be utterly impossible for any one to get any Sin truly and spiritually Mortified.

3\. Draw out the Particulars whereby this is to be done; in the whole carrying on this #emph[Consideration,] that it is not of the #emph[Doctrine of Mortification] in general, but only in reference to the particular Case before proposed, that I am treating.

=== I. What It Is to Mortify a Sin

==== 1. Negatively: What It Is Not

1\. To #emph[Mortify] a Sin, is not utterly to #emph[kill,] root it out, and destroy it, that it should have no more hold at all, nor #emph[residence] in our hearts. It is true, this is that which is aimed at, but this is not in this Life to be accomplished. There is no man that truly sets himself to #emph[mortify] any Sin, but he #emph[aims] at, intends, desires its utter destruction; that it should leave neither #emph[Root] nor #emph[Fruit] in the Heart or Life. He would so #emph[kill] it, that it should never #emph[move] or #emph[stir] any more; #emph[cry] or #emph[call, seduce] or #emph[tempt] to Eternity. Its #emph[not being,] is the thing aimed at. Now though doubtless there may by the Spirit and Grace of Christ, a #emph[wonderful success,] and Eminency of #emph[victory] against any sin be attained; so that a man may have almost constant #emph[triumph] over it; yet an utter #emph[killing] and #emph[destruction] of it, that it should #emph[not be,] is not in this Life to be expected. This #emph[Paul] assures us of, Phil 3:12. #emph[Not as though I had already attained, or were already perfect.] He was a #emph[choice Saint,] a pattern for Believers, who in #emph[Faith] and #emph[Love,] and all the Fruits of the Spirit, had not his Fellow in the world; and on that account ascribes #emph[perfection] to himself, in comparison of others, v. 15. Yet he had not #emph[attained,] he was not #emph[perfect,] but was following after: still #emph[a vile body] he had, and we have, that must be changed by the great power of Christ at last: v. 21. This we would have, but God sees it best for us, that we should be #emph[complete] in nothing in ourselves; that in #emph[all things] we might be #emph[complete] in Christ, which is best for us, Col 2:10.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. I think I need not say, it is not the #emph[dissimulation] of a Sin; when a man on some outward respects forsakes the Practice of any sin, men perhaps may #emph[look] on him as a changed man; God knows that to his former #emph[iniquity] he hath added cursed #emph[hypocrisy,] and is got in a safer path to Hell than he was in before. He hath got another Heart than he had, that is more cunning, not a new #emph[Heart,] that is more holy.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. The Mortification of Sin consists not in the Improvement of a #emph[quiet, sedate] Nature. Some men have an Advantage by their #emph[natural Constitution,] so far, as that they are not #emph[exposed] to such #emph[violence] of #emph[unruly] passions, and #emph[tumultuous] Affections, as many others are. Let now these men #emph[cultivate] and improve their #emph[Natural Frame] and Temper, by #emph[Discipline,] Consideration and #emph[Prudence,] and they may seem to themselves and others, very #emph[mortified men,] when perhaps their Hearts are a standing #emph[Sink] of all Abominations; some man is never so much troubled all his Life perhaps with #emph[anger] and #emph[passion,] nor doth trouble #emph[others,] as another is almost every day; and yet the latter have done more to the #emph[Mortification] of the sin, than the #emph[former.] Let not such persons try their Mortification by such things as their #emph[natural Temper] gives no Life or Vigor to: let them bring themselves to #emph[self-denial, unbelief, envy,] or some such #emph[spiritual sin,] and they will have a better #emph[view] of themselves.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. A sin is not #emph[Mortified,] when it is only #emph[diverted. Simon Magus] for a season left his #emph[Sorceries]\; but his #emph[Covetousness] and #emph[Ambition] that set him on work, remained still, and would have been acting another way: therefore #emph[Peter] tells him, #emph[I perceive thou art in the gall of bitterness]\; notwithstanding the Profession thou hast made, notwithstanding thy Relinquishment of thy #emph[Sorceries,] thy #emph[lust] is as #emph[powerful] as ever in thee: the #emph[same lust,] only the streams of it are diverted: it now exerts and puts forth itself another way, but it is the old #emph[gall] of Bitterness still. A man may be #emph[sensible] of a #emph[lust,] set himself against the #emph[eruptions] of it, take care that it shall not #emph[break] forth, as it hath done; but in the mean time suffer the same #emph[corrupted habit] to vent itself some other way. As he who #emph[heals] and #emph[skins] a running Sore, thinks himself cured, but in the mean time his Flesh #emph[festereth] by the Corruption of the same #emph[humor,] and breaks out in another place. And this #emph[diversion,] with the Alterations that attend it, often befalls men, on Accounts wholly foreign unto Grace; change of the Course of Life that a man was in; of Relations, Interests, Designs, may effect it; yea the very Alterations in men's Constitutions, occasioned by a natural Progress in the Course of their Lives, may produce such Changes as these; men in Age do not usually persist in the pursuit of #emph[youthful lusts,] although they have never mortified any one of them. And the same is the Case of #emph[bartering] of Lusts; and leaving to serve #emph[one,] that a man may serve #emph[another.] He that changes #emph[pride] for #emph[worldliness, sensuality] for #emph[Pharisaism, vanity] in Himself to the #emph[contempt] of Others; let him not think that he hath Mortified the sin that he seems to have left. He hath changed his #emph[Master,] but is a #emph[Servant] still.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[5\. #emph[Occasional] Conquests of Sin do not amount to a #emph[Mortifying] of it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[There are two #emph[Occasions] or Seasons, wherein a man who is contending with any #emph[sin,] may seem to himself to have #emph[mortified it.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(1.) When it hath had some sad Eruption to the disturbance of his #emph[Peace,] terror of his #emph[Conscience,] dread of #emph[Scandal,] and evident provocation of #emph[God.] This awakens and stirs up all that is in the man, and #emph[amazes] him; fills him with #emph[abhorrency] of sin, and #emph[himself] for it; sends him to God, makes him #emph[cry out] as for Life, to abhor his Lust as #emph[Hell,] and to set himself against it. The whole man, #emph[spiritual] and #emph[natural] being now awaked, Sin #emph[shrinks] in its head, #emph[appears] not, but lies as dead before him. As when one that hath drawn nigh to an #emph[Army] in the Night, and hath killed a #emph[principal person]\; instantly the #emph[guards] awake, men are #emph[roused] up, and strict Inquiry is made after the Enemy; who in the mean time, until the noise and tumult be over, hides himself, or lies like one that is dead, yet with firm Resolution to do the like mischief again, upon the like Opportunity. Upon the sin among the #emph[Corinthians,] see how they #emph[muster] up themselves for the #emph[surprisal] and Destruction of it, 2 #emph[Epist.] ch. 7 v. 11. So it is in a person, when a breach hath been made upon his Conscience, Quiet, perhaps #emph[Credit,] by his Lust, in some #emph[Eruption] of Actual sin; #emph[Carefulness, Indignation, Desire, Fear, Revenge,] are all set on work about it, and against it, and Lust is #emph[quiet] for a season, being run down before them; but when the #emph[hurry] is over, and the #emph[Inquest] past, the #emph[Thief] appears again alive, and is as busy as ever at his work.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(2.) In a time of some #emph[Judgment, Calamity,] or pressing #emph[Affliction]\; the Heart is then taken up with Thoughts and #emph[Contrivances] of #emph[flying] from the present #emph[troubles, fears] and #emph[dangers:] This, as a #emph[convinced person] concludes, is to be done, only by #emph[relinquishment of Sin,] which gains peace with God. It is the Anger of God in every Affliction that #emph[galls] a Convinced person. To be quit of this, men resolve at such times against their sins. #emph[Sin shall never more have any place in them; they will never again give up themselves to the service of it.] Accordingly Sin is quiet, stirs not, seems to be Mortified; not indeed that it hath received any one #emph[wound,] but merely because the Soul hath possessed its Faculties whereby it should exert itself, with thoughts inconsistent with the #emph[motions] thereof; which when they are laid aside, Sin returns again to its former Life and Vigor. So they Ps 78:32 unto 38. Are a full Instance and Description of this Frame of spirit whereof I speak.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[For all this they sinned still, and believed not for his wondrous works:]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Therefore their days did he consume in vanity, and their years in trouble.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[When he slew them, then they sought him, and they returned, and inquired early after God.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[And they remembered that God was their rock, and the high God their Redeemer.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Nevertheless they did flatter him with their mouth, and they lied unto him with their tongues.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[For their heart was not right with him, neither were they steadfast in his Covenant.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I no way doubt, but that when they #emph[sought,] and #emph[returned,] and inquired #emph[early] after God, they did it with full #emph[purpose of heart,] as to the #emph[relinquishment] of their sins: It is expressed in the word #emph[returned.] To #emph[turn] or #emph[return] to the Lord, is by a Relinquishment of sin. This they did #emph[early,] with #emph[earnestness,] and #emph[diligence]\; but yet their sin was #emph[unmortified] for all this, vv. 36, 37 and this is the state of many #emph[humiliations] in the days of #emph[Affliction]\; and a great Deceit in the Hearts of Believers themselves, lies oftentimes herein.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[These and many other ways there are, whereby poor Souls deceive themselves, and suppose they have #emph[mortified] their #emph[Lusts,] when they #emph[live] and are Mighty, and on every Occasion break forth to their disturbance and disquietness.]]
